    id: 'w8-interfaces',
    kind: 'concept',
    title: 'Concept: interfaces, interface management and the N² matrix',
    paragraphs: [
      'What an interface is. MITRE defines interface management as defining, controlling and communicating the information needed to let unrelated objects — systems, services, equipment, software and data — work together. Most new systems need external interfaces, and interface management starts at design and continues through operations (R57 p.410). MITRE writes about ICT: interfaces are the functional and physical connections at the boundaries of ICT systems designed to interoperate with other systems. There are many kinds: communications, signalling, service, data, hardware and software interfaces, and APIs. The purpose of managing them is to provide all the design information needed for separately designed items to work together, and to use the interface’s performance, functional and physical attributes as constraints on design changes (R57 p.411). SEBoK’s physical-architecture definition is broader still. A physical interface binds two system elements, like a link or connector. In service and enterprise systems the interface may be a protocol, a procedure or a document rather than a piece of hardware (R20c p.597).',
      'Typing interfaces. The course types every IF by what it exchanges. A physical interface carries energy, mass or mechanical load (IF-005, aircraft ↔ charger power). An information interface carries data (IF-008, booking platform ↔ operations centre). An organisational interface is an agreed procedure or responsibility between organisations or teams (IF-004, the operations ↔ maintenance work-order handover). Interfaces to EXT elements are also flagged as external (RB-7.3). This three-way typing is the course’s convention. It is not a SEBoK or MITRE taxonomy: in SEBoK all three are “physical interfaces” in the sense of binding two elements (R20c p.597). The typing matters because each type is managed differently (instructor guidance): energy interfaces are qualified by test; information interfaces need a data definition and a service level; organisational interfaces need an agreement with an owner on each side. It also decides what an interface can carry. Course coverage rule (instructor-defined; the N² tool and the tracer apply it): an information flow is carried only by an information interface, and an energy or material flow only by a physical one. An organisational interface carries no flow by itself — it fixes who does what and when, not how the data get across — so a report produced under a procedure still needs an information interface, or a recorded relay path over information interfaces. MITRE’s advice is written for ICT interfaces; the course applies it to all three types (instructor extension). Establish the interface-description framework early, during design. Describe every interface with the same set of attributes, including the business process or data flow it supports and not only its technical details. Agree service levels for interface performance (R57 p.413). Use an interface control working group for issues that engineer-to-engineer contact cannot resolve (R57 p.414). MITRE also warns that loosely coupled architectures need more governance, not less, because interfaces sprawl (R57 p.412).',
      'The N² matrix. As a design aim, SEBoK’s physical-architecture article says a candidate architecture may aim at reducing the number of physical interfaces (R20c p.599). Its practices table adds that modularity means maximum consistency inside an element and a minimum of physical interfaces with the outside, and recommends focusing on interfaces rather than on elements at abstract levels (R20c p.603). The N² matrix is the tool that makes interfaces countable. SEBoK’s System Integration article (required extract R22 p.630–631) names the N-squared diagram as one of the most basic methods for defining integration aggregates and the order of integration. In the integration context the coupling matrix is reorganised to group implemented elements into aggregates so that the number of interfaces to be verified between aggregates is minimised, and the matrix then helps locate faults (R22 p.630–631). That is the integration analogue of the design aim, not the design aim itself: R22 is about elements that already exist.',
      'Course N² conventions (instructor-defined). Elements sit on the diagonal. In the interface N², an off-diagonal cell lists the IF IDs joining two elements, with the type. Interfaces are two-ended, so each IF is written once, above the diagonal, and the matrix is read as symmetric. In the functional N² the matrix is directed: row = producing function, column = consuming function, and the cell lists the flow. Counting an interface N² gives the number of interfaces, the number of each type, and the number per element (its degree). Each interface has two ends, so the degrees always sum to twice the number of interfaces — a useful arithmetic check.',
    ],
    citations: [{ reading: 'R57', pages: '410–414' }, { reading: 'R20c', pages: '597, 599, 603' }, { reading: 'R22', pages: '630–631' }],
