      'The five reference information items are the S04 Lab 8 list. For each one the table records the producer, the consumer, the decision supported and the interface the flow crosses. Read INFO-003 Maintenance Data from left to right. The maintenance facility (PHY-005) and the chargers’ charge-session records (PHY-003) produce it. Dispatch at the operations centre (PHY-004) and the predictive-maintenance capability AI-001, hosted at the maintenance facility, consume it. The decision it supports is release or withhold an aircraft, which is what REQ-STK-006 and REQ-SYS-008 constrain. In R21’s terms (p.512), the raw fault codes and cell temperatures are data. The go/no-go status and AI-001’s ranked list of at-risk packs are information, because they have been analysed and interpreted for the release decision.',
      'Tracing finds gaps that neither a function list nor an element list would show. When the review traced the first version of the reference (IF-001..009), five producer–consumer pairs had no information interface joining them. Three had no interface at all: INFO-002 PHY-001 → PHY-005 (flight data and fault reports to maintenance), INFO-003 PHY-003 → PHY-004 (charge-session records to dispatch) and INFO-004 EXT-003 → PHY-004 (weather to decision support). Two were joined only by an organisational agreement: INFO-001’s boarding timestamps PHY-002 → PHY-004 across IF-009, the pad-slot agreement, and INFO-003’s maintenance records PHY-005 → PHY-004 across IF-004, the work-order handover. An agreement says who reports what; it does not carry the data. Each gap needed an information interface of its own or a recorded relay over information interfaces. The reference now has an interface for each, IF-014, IF-013, IF-010, IF-011 and IF-012, and the tracer reports no advisory on it. Two of the choices are worth copying. INFO-002’s fault reports could have been relayed (aircraft to operations centre over IF-002, then on to maintenance over IF-012), but the direct IF-013 keeps the no-go path off the dispatch platform (R-007). INFO-003’s charge-session records for AI-001 do take a relay, PHY-003 → PHY-004 → PHY-005 over IF-010 and IF-012, and the relay is recorded on INFO-003 so that a reviewer can check every hop. The tracer checks element-to-element pairs only, so the recorded relay is what makes that route reviewable: an unrecorded relay cannot be told apart from a gap. INFO-005 Financial Data is the opposite case: its consumer is a decision record (DEC-001), not an element, which is legitimate for an item that feeds the board’s capacity decision.',
